# Filled onboarding intake files (scripts/onboard-company.ts input). A filled intake is
# a plaintext list of live AHJ/utility portal passwords and security answers, with no
# encryption and no access control — it must NEVER be committed. The rule is still to keep
# the filled copy OUTSIDE the repo (docs/ONBOARDING.md); these patterns are only the net
# under that rule.
# "intake/" is unanchored so it matches an ~/intake-style working folder at any depth.
intake/
*intake*.json
# The two patterns above cannot see a filled copy saved under the company's OWN name
# (cascade-ridge.json) — which is exactly the name docs/ONBOARDING.md uses in its example,
# and the repo root and docs/ are where such a file lands by accident. So both are closed by
# extension, not by guessing filenames: no JSON at the repo root, and no JSON under docs/.
# Neither directory holds a tracked .json today except the three configs negated below.
# ADDING A NEW ROOT-LEVEL OR docs/ JSON FILE? Add a negation line for it here, deliberately.
# A silent "why won't git add my file" is the cheap failure; committing a live portal
# password is the expensive one.
/*.json
!/package.json
!/package-lock.json
!/tsconfig.json
!/tsconfig.*.json
docs/**/*.json
# ...but the blank template itself stays tracked. Keep this negation LAST.
!docs/onboarding/intake-template.json

# The draft ledger is an operational record of real portal drafts — keep it OUT of git
# (it names live accounts and portal references) but never delete it.
data/portal-drafts.jsonl
IRL NOTES/
